    26	---
    27	
    28	**Objective.** Compactness is the hypothesis under which an infinite covering
    29	argument collapses to a finite one. This page defines it for an arbitrary
    30	topological space, proves what it gives — closedness in a Hausdorff space,
    31	separation of disjoint compact sets, attainment of extreme values, automatic
    32	continuity of an inverse — and settles how it behaves under the constructions of
    33	general topology: subspaces, products of any size, continuous images, and the
    34	adjunction of a single point at infinity.
    35	
    36	**Compactness of a subset is intrinsic.** [[def-compact-space]] defines a compact
    37	space by open covers and calls a subset compact when the subspace it carries is
    38	compact. That is a statement about the topological subspace, so it is invariant
    39	under homeomorphism. For the same underlying set in another ambient, the answer
    40	is guaranteed to agree when the induced topology agrees. The bridge to
    41	the working form is [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]]: a subset is
    42	compact exactly when every family of ambient open sets covering it has finitely
    43	many members that already cover it, in the indexed form as well as the unindexed
    44	one. Almost every proof below uses the indexed form, because a cover is normally
    45	produced by a rule attaching an open set to each point or to each index, and a
    46	bare set of open sets forgets the rule. The same reading was already fixed for
    47	metric spaces, and [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]] shows that
    48	the two developments describe one notion, which is what makes the whole metric
    49	theory of compactness available here once a metric inducing the topology is named.
    50	
    51	**What compactness gives.** [[thm-compact-iff-fip]] converts the covering
    52	condition into a statement about closed sets with the finite intersection
    53	property, which is the form used whenever a common point has to be produced.
    54	[[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]] and
    55	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]] are the two halves of the
    56	relationship between compactness and closedness, and the second carries more than
    57	its name: in a Hausdorff space a point and a disjoint compact set, and two
    58	disjoint compact sets, lie in disjoint open sets. Those separation clauses are
    59	proved **choice-free**, by collecting the family of all open sets that work
    60	rather than choosing one for each point, and they are what later pages use to
    61	separate closed sets in a compact Hausdorff space.
    62	[[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]] collects the payoff: a continuous image
    63	of a compact space is compact, a continuous real-valued function on a nonempty
    64	compact space attains a maximum and a minimum, and a continuous bijection from a
    65	compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism.
    66	
    67	**Products.** [[lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor]] is the step that makes a
    68	product of two compact spaces compact, and it is proved with no choice principle
    69	at all by indexing its cover with pairs of open sets;
    70	[[thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces]] runs the induction. For an arbitrary
    71	index set the argument changes character: [[thm-alexander-subbase-lemma]] reduces
    72	compactness to covers by members of a fixed subbasis, using Zorn's lemma, and
    73	[[thm-tychonoff]] then follows because a subbasic cover of a product restricts
    74	one coordinate at a time. Tychonoff's theorem implies the Axiom of Choice, so no
    75	proof of it can be free.
    76	
    77	**Weaker cousins, and what each implication costs.**
    78	[[def-compactness-variants]] introduces countable compactness, the Lindelöf
    79	property, sequential compactness, limit point compactness, $\sigma$-compactness
    80	and relative compactness, and identifies those of them that the metric
