    37	## Statement
    38	
    39	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), let
    40	$A \subseteq X$ and let $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ be the subspace
    41	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). Then:
    42	
    43	1. **Compactness read in the ambient space.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$
    44	   ([[def-compact-space]]), that is $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact space, if
    45	   and only if for every family $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ with
    46	   $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    47	   $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $A \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$,
    48	   or else $A = \varnothing$.
    49	2. **The same in indexed form.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$ if and only if
    50	   for every set $I$ and every family $(U_i)_{i \in I}$ of open subsets of $X$
    51	   with $A \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} U_i$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    52	   indices $i_0, \dots, i_n \in I$ with
    53	   $A \subseteq U_{i_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{i_n}$, or else $A = \varnothing$.
    54	
    55	Claim 2 is the form used by almost every later proof on this page, because a
    56	cover is usually produced by a rule that attaches an open set to each point or to
    57	each index, and a set of open sets forgets that rule. No choice principle is used
    58	anywhere below; the one place a selection is made is over a finite index set, and
    59	[[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
    60	
    61	## Facts & Assumptions
    62	
    63	**Given:** A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$, a subset $A \subseteq X$, and the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ with $\mathcal{T}_A = \{\, U \cap A : U \in \mathcal{T} \,\}$.
    64	
    65	[L1] A subset of $A$ is open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ exactly when it is the trace $U \cap A$ of a set $U$ open in $X$, this being the definition of the subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    66	
    67	[L2] $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is compact exactly when every family of sets open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ whose union is $A$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $A$; a family is finite when it is empty or listable as $\{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$ ([[def-compact-space]]).
    68	
    69	[L3] A function with domain a natural number all of whose values are nonempty sets has a choice function, and this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]).
    70	
    71	## Proof
    72	
    73	**Proof technique:** direct.
    74	
    75	1.1 Suppose $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is compact, let $I$ be a set and let $(U_i)_{i \in I}$ be open subsets of $X$ with $A \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} U_i$; then each $U_i \cap A$ is open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ and $\mathcal{V} := \{\, U_i \cap A : i \in I \,\}$ is a family of open subsets of $A$ whose union is $A$. [L1, L2]
    76	
    77	2.1 If $A = \varnothing$ the conclusion of claim 2 holds by its second alternative, so assume $A \ne \varnothing$; then $\mathcal{V}$ is an open cover of $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$, and compactness yields $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $V_0, \dots, V_n \in \mathcal{V}$ with $A = V_0 \cup \dots \cup V_n$. [L2, step 1.1]
    78	
    79	3.1 For each $j \le n$ the set $S_j := \{\, i \in I : U_i \cap A = V_j \,\}$ is nonempty by the definition of $\mathcal{V}$, and $j \mapsto S_j$ is a function with domain the natural number $\sigma(n)$, so a choice function for its values supplies $i_0, \dots, i_n \in I$ with $U_{i_j} \cap A = V_j$ for every $j \le n$. [L3, step 2.1]
    80	
    81	4.1 Hence $A = V_0 \cup \dots \cup V_n = (U_{i_0} \cap A) \cup \dots \cup (U_{i_n} \cap A) \subseteq U_{i_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{i_n}$, which is the conclusion of claim 2 for the family $(U_i)_{i \in I}$, so the forward implication of claim 2 holds. [step 2.1, step 3.1]
    82	
    83	5.1 The converse of claim 2 remains, the forward implication having been settled at step 4.1; so assume the displayed condition, let $\mathcal{G}$ be a family of sets open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ with union $A$, and put $\mathcal{W} := \{\, U \in \mathcal{T} : U \cap A \in \mathcal{G} \,\}$, a family cut out by a property and indexed by itself. [L1, step 4.1, construct]
    84	
    85	6.1 $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{W}$: given $a \in A$ there is $G \in \mathcal{G}$ with $a \in G$, and by [L1] there is $U$ open in $X$ with $U \cap A = G$; that $U$ lies in $\mathcal{W}$ and contains $a$. [L1, step 5.1]
    86	
    87	7.1 If $A = \varnothing$ the empty subfamily of $\mathcal{G}$ covers $A$; otherwise the assumed condition applied to the family $\mathcal{W}$ indexed by itself gives $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $W_0, \dots, W_m \in \mathcal{W}$ with $A \subseteq W_0 \cup \dots \cup W_m$. [step 5.1, step 6.1]
    88	
    89	8.1 Putting $G_j := W_j \cap A$ for $j \le m$ gives members of $\mathcal{G}$ with $A = (W_0 \cap A) \cup \dots \cup (W_m \cap A) = G_0 \cup \dots \cup G_m$, so $\mathcal{G}$ has a finite subcover and $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is compact. [L2, step 7.1]
    90	
    91	9.1 Claim 2 is proved by steps 4.1 and 8.1, and claim 1 is the special case of claim 2 in which $I = \mathcal{U}$ is a family of open subsets of $X$ and $U_i := i$, the conclusion of claim 2 then naming members of $\mathcal{U}$ itself. [step 4.1, step 8.1] ∎
    92	
    93	## Remarks
    94	
    95	**Why the ambient reading needed a proof at all.** A subset $A$ of $X$ carries two candidate notions of open cover: families of sets open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$, and families of sets open in $X$ whose union contains $A$. The trace description of the subspace topology is what turns one into the other, and it shows that compactness can be checked using ambient open sets for this fixed induced topology. Another ambient is guaranteed to give the same answer when it induces the same topology on $A$; if the induced topology changes, the answer may change. Every later item on this page that covers a subset by ambient open sets is using claim 1 or claim 2, and says so.
    96	
    97	**The traces do not remember their sources.** A single relatively open $V$ is usually the trace of many different ambient open sets, and that is exactly why step 3.1 has to recover indices at all. Recovering infinitely many at once would be a choice principle; recovering finitely many is not, and the proof is arranged so that only finitely many are ever needed.
    98	
    99	**The metric statement of the same fact is [[lem-compactness-is-intrinsic]]**, whose claims 2 and 3 are claims 1 and 2 above with the open subsets of a metric space in place of the members of an abstract topology. Its proof carries an extra first claim, that relative openness in a metric subspace is a trace, which here is the definition of the subspace topology and so needs no argument. Neither statement is used in the proof of the other; that the two agree is [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
